{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'collab/yang-clojure/1794860000000-engineer-d1-r23-launch-closure-retention-replay.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# D1 r23 — expansion guard covers oracle initialization\n\nWorktree mirror; no commit. **Partial, below the items 1–3 checkpoint.** The requested launch configuration, complete dependency closure, durable reconstruction package, package-pinned expansion determination, and isolated replay remain unfinished. No D1 verification or historical runtime attestation is claimed. Existing admissions, outcomes, roots, events, bounds and row-lane restrictions remain unchanged.\n\n## Local probe increment\n\n`test/yang/correspondence_launch_probe.py` now loads `clojure.test` and `yin.vm.macro`, arms `expand` and `expand-batch` to throw, and only then requires `yang.latte-oracle-test` and runs `test-d1-amended-replay-closed-store`. This extends the r22 guard over oracle namespace initialization as well as the test itself. The macro namespace must be initialized before its Vars can be redefined; that initialization is not guarded. Other possible expansion paths are not intercepted. The probe is not pinned to a package and cannot establish historical expansion behavior.\n\nThe same probe removes classpath directories 5–7 and copies jars 76–77 without their two observed native entries, retaining all other classpath entries and order. On this machine it passed **1 test, 6 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, including with the guard active during oracle loading. It still resolves source and dependencies from the checkout and local caches, runs installed `/usr/bin/java`, and uses the checkout working directory. It neither proves complete closure nor determines necessity for a future package entrypoint or broader fixture coverage. Script SHA-256: `28391a753573a74a64feddf2ec07d706460e8c08621ece2dda11d3f7f5f41ae9b7991`.\n\n## Remaining work\n\nThe r19 runtime object, r20 jar bundle, and r21 directory bundle remain scratch observations, not a durable package. The existing manifest is a draft inventory, not an enforced launch plan. Specify and enforce the exact runtime, package-relative ordered classpath and resource resolution, module path, working directory, JVM options, properties, environment, locale/encoding, initialization, pipeline and supported platform. Establish the source/resource and binary closure for that actual entrypoint, including refusal of undeclared inputs. Retain and identify the package with an owner and discovery mechanism; verify transitive availability after process exit on a scratch copy. The local exclusions above are evidence for the tested path, not a complete closure determination.\n\nThen use the pinned composition and checked execution path to determine expansion applicability, or capture the applicable context and events. Fetch and verify the retained package and run a fresh process resolving compiler code from it, not the invoking checkout; reconstruct adaptation and assembly through compilation and stamping and compare all claimed outputs. Preserve the distinction between later reconstruction and historical provenance, and do not invent admissions or events. Independent classification, image derivation, trace and bounded-run/publication obligations also remain.\n\nThe third architect ruling, available prior findings and declarations were consulted. The two earlier ruling filenames and continuation-4 remain unavailable under the supplied names, as reported in earlier findings; no substitute ruling is inferred.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe local probe passed as above. Python byte compilation passed with its cache in `/private/tmp`. clj-kondo on the correspondence manifest/dependency files, manifest test and oracle test reported 0 errors and 0 warnings. `git diff --check` passed. Frozen `slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite, tagged lane, corpus replay, publication or commit was run.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'docs/design/datom.world.md'\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'collab/yang-clojure/1794850000000-engineer-d1-r22-launch-closure-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'collab/yang-clojure/1794850000000-engineer-d1-r22-launch-closure-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# D1 r22 — targeted launch and expansion probe\n\nWorktree mirror only; no commit. **Partial, below the requested items 1–3 checkpoint.** A fresh local JVM completed one existing static reconstruction test while three observed generated-parser directories and two observed Netty native entries were unavailable on its classpath. A second run additionally made the two row-native expansion entrypoints throw if invoked during the test; it passed. These observations narrow the launch and applicability questions for that test. They do not constitute a complete build plan, executable manifest, durable package, expansion declaration, isolated package replay, historical runtime attestation, or D1 verification.\n\n## Probe and observations\n\n`test/yang/correspondence_launch_probe.py` (SHA-256 `b8a6d3f067df38508d7c29e22fc3433d82317bd6bb4feb24b0a94b46338396b7`) obtains the local `clojure -Spath -M:test` classpath, requires 154 entries, and removes indices 5–7: the Python, PHP and TypeScript generated parser class directories. It creates temporary copies of the jars at indices 76 and 77, excluding exactly `META-INF/native/libnetty_transport_native_epoll_x86_64.so` and `META-INF/native/libnetty_transport_native_kqueue_x86_64.jnilib`, and refuses unexpected native-entry names. The other classpath entries and their order are preserved. The copies are test instrumentation, not proposed retained dependency bytes.\n\nThe script starts `/usr/bin/java` with `-Xss64m`, `-Dfile.encoding=UTF-8`, English/US language properties, and a deliberately small environment (`PATH`, temporary `HOME` and `TMPDIR`, `LANG=C`, `LC_ALL=C`). On this machine that launcher reported OpenJDK 21.0.2. The working directory remains the invoking checkout; classpath entries still point to the checkout and local dependency caches, and the installed Java and host remain available. It invokes only `test-d1-amended-replay-closed-store`, whose own assertions check one `:beta-eq-false` fresh admission, compilation, stamping, retained-input readback and producer-side reconstruction. The probe requires exactly one test and six passing assertions. It is reproducible locally with `python3 test/yang/correspondence_launch_probe.py` when the expected local classpath layout exists; the `clojure -Spath` setup step itself is not environment-isolated.\n\nTwo runs passed 1 test / 6 assertions each, with no failures or errors. The first removed the directories and native entries; the second, represented by the current script, also redefined `yin.vm.macro/expand` and `expand-batch` to throw during the test. The namespace was loaded before those redefinitions. The result shows that neither of these entrypoints was called during this tested compilation/replay. It does not exclude other expansion paths or prove behavior during namespace initialization, for other fixtures, or for the historical admission. The direct composition calls `d1-assemble`, `yang/compile-program` with `true`, and `kernel-stamp-sites`, while the frontend's namespace documentation distinguishes its rewrites from row-native macro lookup. Together these are useful evidence for the candidate bypass, not the ruling's package-pinned checked execution and complete applicability declaration.\n\nFor this one path, the three observed generated parser directories and those two jar-embedded native binaries were not required while absent from the declared classpath. This does not establish whether a future reconstruction entrypoint, its full fixture/adaptation coverage, initialization, or another supported host needs them. The test does not intercept all filesystem, dynamic-library or resource accesses, and does not prove the absence of other undeclared dependencies. It therefore would be premature to exclude these inputs from a claimed complete plan or to call all native-library necessity resolved.\n\n## Remaining work\n\nThe r19 normalized JDK archive, r20 jar store, and r21 directory store are still present under `target/`, but remain scratch observations. No owner or durable availability commitment has been established and no package was retained there under the r7 proposal. The prior source snapshots are not proved identical to the current compiler and helpers. A complete versioned launch must specify and enforce the entrypoint, exact package-relative classpath/resource ordering, module path, runtime, JVM and property values, environment, locale/encoding, working directory, initialization, supported host and allowed facilities. It needs verified source and resource inputs and a build/load plan, not just the probe's local command. Confirm generated and native exclusions against that exact plan.\n\nThe isolated entrypoint must fetch and verify addressed package artifacts after process exit, launch with compiler code resolved from the package and checkout/caches unavailable, reconstruct fixture adaptation and assembly through compilation and stamping, and compare all claimed outputs. It must substantiate expansion bypass for the pinned path or retain the applicable event/context closure, while preserving the separate uncertainty of historical expansion and admission. Independent classification, image derivations, trace obligations and bounded-run/publication work remain. Existing bounds, row-lane restriction, frozen v1 evidence and retained admission coordinates are unchanged.\n\nThe earlier two ruling filenames and continuation-4 were not found under the supplied names in this worktree; no substitute text is inferred. The third ruling, available findings, draft bounds and cc3 declaration were consulted.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe two probe runs described above passed. `python3 -m py_compile` passed with its cache directed to `/private/tmp`; the first attempt tried to create Apple's default cache outside the permitted workspace and was denied. clj-kondo on the manifest/dependency files, manifest test and oracle test reported 0 errors and 0 warnings. `git diff --check` passed. Frozen `slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 is still `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite, tagged lane, corpus replay, publication, or commit was run.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'test/yang/correspondence_snapshot_probe_test.py'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'test/yang/correspondence_snapshot_probe_test.py'\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Safety checks for the exploratory snapshot extractor.\"\"\"\nimport hashlib\nimport importlib.util\nimport io\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport tarfile\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\n\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('snapshot_probe', Path(__file__).with_name('correspondence_snapshot_probe.py'))\nprobe = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(probe)\n\n\nclass ExtractionTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def archive(self, root, name, link=None):\n        path = root / 'object.tar'\n        with tarfile.open(path, 'w') as tar:\n            item = tarfile.TarInfo(name)\n            if link is None:\n                item.size = 2\n                tar.addfile(item, io.BytesIO(b'ok'))\n            else:\n                item.type = tarfile.SYMTYPE\n                item.linkname = link\n                tar.addfile(item)\n        return path, {'size': path.stat().st_size,\n                      'sha256': hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()}\n\n    def test_valid_and_tampered(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:\n            root = Path(tmp)\n            obj, record = self.archive(root, 'content/file')\n            dest = root / 'out'\n            dest.mkdir()\n            probe.extract_checked(obj, record, dest)\n            self.assertEqual(b'ok', (dest / 'content/file').read_bytes())\n            record['sha256'] = '0' * 64\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n                probe.extract_checked(obj, record, dest)\n\n    def test_rejects_escape_and_unsupported_link(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:\n            root = Path(tmp)\n            dest = root / 'out'\n            dest.mkdir()\n            for name, link in [('content/../../escape', None), ('content/link', '../../escape'),\n                               ('content/link', '/absolute')]:\n                obj, record = self.archive(root, name, link)\n                with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n                    probe.extract_checked(obj, record, dest)\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'collab/yang-clojure/1794840000000-engineer-d1-r21-closure-launch-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat 'collab/yang-clojure/1794840000000-engineer-d1-r21-closure-launch-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# D1 r21 — observed classpath directory archives in an addressed bundle\n\nWorktree mirror only; no commit. **Partial, below the requested items 1–3 checkpoint.** The ten observed classpath directory archives have now been retained as bytes in a separate scratch Jing bundle and checked after copy and reopen. This is an inventory checkpoint, not a complete reconstruction package, launch configuration, durable retention, expansion-applicability determination, isolated replay, historical runtime attestation, or D1 verification. Existing admissions, outcomes, roots, events, bounds and row-lane restrictions are unchanged.\n\n## Directory archive checkpoint\n\nThe r19 inventory records ten directory entries, at classpath indices 0–7, 13 and 15. `test/yang/correspondence_r21_directory_inventory.edn` records their ordered indices, descriptive observed paths, archive lengths and SHA-256 values. Its SHA-256 is `e5bf742bc8fc409324e66653db6c9fd504d344f5c069483b55b76ff6d5a4bdd9`. The tar objects are the r18/r19 observed snapshots, not a new capture of the current checkout. Before storing, the new helper checks each object's complete archive length and SHA-256, then stores the exact bytes under `directories/NNN.tar`, preserving gaps in the ordered classpath index. After the writer exited, another process fetched the bundle from a copy of the store and checked all ten names, lengths and SHA-256 values against the inventory.\n\nThe bundle address is `:segment/blake3-f4a59e417248e669f28af9273786dc966695fee3c8069f5b762880ffba8fffe0`, in `target/d1-r21-directory-store/content.jing` (approximately 48 MiB). Its file SHA-256 before reopening is `529f825ff42fb9361bfe635520537fb2fcd71eb4fa806dfcdc6e0011109f6af7`. The helper is `test/yang/correspondence_directory_bundle.clj`. Verification used a copy of `content.jing` in `target/d1-r21-directory-reopen`, not an open on the original:\n\n```\nclojure -M test/yang/correspondence_directory_bundle.clj verify target/d1-r21-directory-reopen test/yang/correspondence_r21_directory_inventory.edn unused :segment/blake3-f4a59e417248e669f28af9273786dc966695fee3c8069f5b762880ffba8fffe0\n```\n\nOne deliberately changed archive length was refused. This verifies the selected bundle and receipt, not safe extraction, closure, or durable availability. The bundle and receipt are separate from the r20 144-jar bundle, and neither has a declared retention owner. No large binary is being represented as a checked-in durable artifact. The tar snapshots preserve their archive contents, including modes and link entries; the helper does not extract or validate them for safe execution.\n\nThe observed ten directories contain 1,559 regular files: 563 `.class` files in the three generated Python, PHP and TypeScript parser directories, and no native-library files or symlink entries. This is inventory, not a determination that these generated classes belong to the source-loaded reconstruction plan. A scan of the 144 captured jars found native library entries in classpath indices 76 and 77 (Netty epoll x86-64 `.so` and kqueue x86-64 `.jnilib`); whether the candidate Darwin arm64 reconstruction loads or needs any native code is not established. An archive-content SHA-256 comparison found the snapshot's `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj` identical to the current file (`5bca303f4976bb7f3c0e9cb7fd5923a34e850310a006fda684af051caea9ad1d`); this single comparison does not establish that all snapshots match the current worktree.\n\n## Launch, closure and expansion findings\n\nThe candidate direct composition is identifiable but not a launch configuration: `d1-assemble` builds the instrumented body and `yang/library-for` prefix, and `kernel-compile` calls `yang/compile-program` with `true` and the assembled options before `kernel-stamp-sites`. `d1-check-reconstruction!` compares assembly, compiled/stamped roots, source hash, site table, metadata, binding and correspondence in the already-loaded producer process. Its compiler and helpers come from the invoking checkout; it is not the requested isolated entrypoint. The current `correspondence-manifest` is explicitly a draft byte inventory. Its launch fields do not enforce environment clearing, locale, encoding, initialization or the actual process invocation, and no executable manifest using it was produced.\n\nThe ordered r19 classpath and the retained normalized JDK identify candidate inputs, but loading the oracle namespace itself performs classpath resource reads, including `yang/latte_oracle/manifest.edn`, and the source has dynamic `requiring-resolve`, evaluation and other reads. Some other paths read relative files and JVM properties. Consequently an empty or guessed environment map and working directory would not be a substantiated complete launch specification. A final plan must identify its exact entrypoint and initialization, package-relative working directory and resource mapping, ordered source/binary paths and resolution rules, retained runtime, supported host, JVM and property values, locale/encoding, explicit environment and allowed host facilities; the launcher must actually apply and check them. The environment must not silently inherit variables such as Java option injection or dependency-cache settings. Determine the reachable source/resource and native dependencies for that entrypoint, include all required bytes, and test with checkout and caches unavailable. Mere presence of the full observed snapshots does not resolve ambient file, native, or initialization dependencies.\n\nThe direct frontend documents that it lowers frontend forms and does not perform row-native macro lookup; the producer composition calls compile and stamping directly. That is useful static evidence for a bypass candidate, not the ruling's checked execution of a pinned composition. No expansion inapplicability declaration is issued. Historical expansion and admission evidence remain separate and cannot be invented from later matching outputs.\n\nThe r7 repository-relative, scratch-copy-before-open location is still a proposal. A real owner and discovery/availability commitment must be identified for the complete package before acceptance. The r19 JDK object, r20 jar bundle, and r21 directory bundle remain scratch. A future isolated replay must fetch and verify all transitive artifacts, resolve compiler code from those fetched bytes, use retained coordinates, rebuild fixture adaptation and assembly through stamping, compare all claimed outputs and refuse undeclared dependencies or mismatches. Independent classification, derivation, trace and bounded-run obligations remain outstanding.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe separate-process scratch-copy reopen checked 10 ordered archive objects; a modified length was rejected. `clojure -M:kondo --lint test/yang/correspondence_directory_bundle.clj` reported 0 errors and 0 warnings. `git diff --check` passed. Frozen `slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite, tagged lane, corpus reconstruction, publication or commit was run. The two earlier architect rulings and continuation-4 remained unavailable under the supplied names as documented in the preceding findings; no substitute ruling is inferred.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
